From c91db66d6385786299e80c9e71e37829c162c5c1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 16:35:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: programme thumbnails and editing, a readable logo, and
 real metadata
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

THUMBNAILS

The column, the API field and the catalogue's display of it already existed;
only the means of putting an image there did not. Admins can now upload one.

The image goes in the database, as slide decks do, because this host has an
ephemeral filesystem and a picture written to disk disappears at the next
restart, quietly filling the catalogue with broken images. It sits in its own
table rather than a column on `programs`: the catalogue lists every programme
at once, and a select-all query would drag megabytes of image across the wire
to render a page that needs only titles.

The media type is read from the file's own leading bytes and never from the
uploader's header, and SVG is refused outright. This is the one place the app
takes a file from a person and serves it back, unauthenticated, from its own
origin — an SVG is XML that may carry script, and a browser shown one from
our domain runs it with our domain's privileges. Sharper thumbnails are not
worth handing the site a way to be turned against the people signed into it.
Fifteen tests cover the sniffing, including that SVG, HTML and a renamed PDF
are all refused.

On upload the programme's thumbnailUrl is set to the serving endpoint with the
row's updated time as a version, so the image caches for a year and a replaced
one still appears immediately.

EDITING

Only status and capacity could be changed. There is now an Edit button opening
the title, focus area, description, start, format and duration — the PATCH
endpoint for them already existed. Status and capacity stay inline on the card,
because they are changed often and one at a time. The card also shows the
current thumbnail, so a missing or wrong one is obvious from the list.

THE LOGO

It now shows the full lockup at every width. Dropping to the mark alone on a
phone meant the site never said its own name on the screen most first-time
visitors arrive on. It is also a step larger, and the main content's top
padding moved with it — that padding clears the fixed header, so leaving it
would have slid the first thing on every page underneath.

METADATA

A shared link now previews as a branded card with the Lab's name, what it does
and the address, generated from the white logo. Plus canonical URL, og:url,
site name, locale and image dimensions.

A web manifest with icons, name and colours, so saving the site to a phone's
home screen produces something that looks and opens like an app. The maskable
icon is inset, or Android crops into the mark.

A sitemap listing the six pages worth indexing, and a robots.txt that keeps
crawlers out of the signed-in surfaces while explicitly allowing /verify/ —
a certificate being publicly checkable is the whole point of it.

AND ONE THING FOUND ON THE WAY

An unhandled error in any route reached Express's default handler, which
answers with an HTML page containing the stack trace and, for a database
failure, the full SQL statement. That page went to whoever made the request,
and the thumbnail endpoint added here is public. Errors now return flat JSON
and the detail goes to the log instead. Verified both halves against a running
server: the response carries no SQL, the log does.

